AUDIT OF THE #583 WORK   (independent re-derivation)
==========================================================================

1. Are we solving the stated problem?

   erdosproblems.com/583, verbatim:
     "Every connected graph on n vertices can be partitioned into at most
      ceil(n/2) edge-disjoint paths."

   PATHS, not trails.  The distinction is the whole difficulty: a connected
   graph with 2t > 0 odd-degree vertices decomposes into exactly t TRAILS by
   an Euler argument, which would make the conjecture easy.  decide5.c tracks
   a VERTEX bitmask (adj[vend] & ~used), so it enforces simple paths, and the
   decider in this file does the same via its `seen` set.
  [PASS] bowtie (two triangles sharing a vertex) needs 2 simple paths, e.g. 0-1-2-3-4 together with 0-2-4  min_paths = 2
     Every degree is even, so ONE closed trail covers it; requiring simple
     paths forces 2. A decider that accepted trails would answer 1 here, so
     this separates the two notions.
  [PASS] and 2 <= ceil(5/2) = 3, so the bowtie satisfies Gallai with room to spare

2. Known values, from the definition.
  [PASS] star K_{1,5}: 6 odd-degree vertices force >= 3 paths, and 3 suffice  min_paths = 3
  [PASS] 5-cycle needs 2: a cycle is not itself a path (written as (i,(i+1)%5), which is the unnormalised form that exposed the bug above)  min_paths = 2
  [PASS] a 5-vertex path needs exactly 1
  [PASS] K_4 needs 2 = ceil(4/2)  min_paths = 2
  [PASS] K_5 needs 3 = ceil(5/2)  min_paths = 3

